226 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (Autism Spectrum Disorder is associated with a typical patterns of functional connectivity in the brain, which can be captured using resting-state fMRI. A common approach in this area is to represent the brain as a graph, using regions of interest as nodes and Pearson correlation between their activity as edges, and train a Graph Neural Network to classify subjects as ASD or Healthy Control based on this graph. However, several aspects of this pipeline remain open to investigation: whether the fixed, correlation-based graph construction limits model performance, whether GNNs meaningfully leverage network structure compared to simpler baselines, whether a learned, task-aware graph performs better than a fixed one, and what kind of patient representation a trained GNN actually learns. This internship's tasks were structured around these questions, starting with a standard classification setup and progressively moving toward graph construction methods and analysis of the model's learned representations.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 152 0 R
/E (Key Challenges :)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 609 0 R
/E (No ground-truth brain graph : The true connectivity structure of the brain is unknown; every graph used is an estimate, and the method used to construct it can affect downstream results)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
